.\" rekta.3: the rekta-core library. Keep EXAMPLES the same as examples/hello.c (make test checks it).
.Dd October 14, 2026
.Dt REKTA 3
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm rekta
.Nd native views for desktop apps, in Rekta Design
.Sh LIBRARY
rekta-core
.Pq librekta, Fl lrekta
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In rekta.h
.Pp
Installed, pkg-config knows what to link:
.Dl cc app.c $(pkg-config --cflags --libs rekta)
The shared library shows only the functions below; its soname carries the
minor version until 1.0, as a minor version may still change the ABI.
Linking the static library by hand, on Linux:
.Dl -lrekta -lwayland-client -lwayland-cursor -lxkbcommon -lrt -lX11 -lXext -lm -lpthread -ldl
on macOS:
.Dl -lrekta -framework Cocoa -framework QuartzCore
on Windows:
.Dl rekta.lib user32.lib gdi32.lib advapi32.lib
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
draws Rekta Design itself, the same pixels on every system, and asks the
system only for a window, input and settings.
An app says what a window shows in a
.Em build
function, from its own state; the library makes the views, keeps them, and
after anything that may have changed the state builds again and changes only
what differs, with the design's motion.
Underneath stands a tree anyone may reach: a
.Em window
shows a
.Em controller ,
a controller owns a
.Em view ,
and views contain views.
The declared UI is the way to write an app; the tree is there for what it
cannot say, such as a custom view.
Layout, spacing, colour, type and motion come from the design tokens, so
an app says what it shows and rarely how.
.Pp
Everything runs on the main thread.
.Fn rk_app_post
is the one function another thread may call.
.Ss Declared UI
A window given
.Fa desc->build
calls it to know what to show:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
static void today(void *user) {
  RK_PANORAMA() RK_SECTION("today") {
    RK_TILES() RK_TILE(rk_ui_fmt("%d", app.finished), .label = "finished");
    RK_BUTTON("build one", .on_press = build_one);
  }
}
.Ed
.Pp
Each macro declares one node: its first argument is its text, the rest are
fields of
.Vt rk_ui
by name, and a field left out is the default.
A container's scope, the block after it, holds what it holds;
.Ic break
or
.Ic continue
inside one ends the scope, and a
.Ic return
or
.Ic goto
out of one is a mistake the build reports.
The kinds: views that hold views
.Pq Dv RK_VSTACK , RK_HSTACK , RK_TILES , RK_ROWS , RK_SCROLL , RK_SPLIT ;
views
.Pq Dv RK_LABEL , RK_BUTTON , RK_TILE , RK_ROW , RK_ICON , RK_PROGRESS , RK_IMAGE , RK_SPACER , RK_FIELD , RK_CHECK , RK_TOGGLE , RK_CHOICES , RK_SEGMENTS , RK_SLIDER , RK_STEPPER , RK_SELECT , RK_DATE , RK_DROP ;
collections
.Pq Dv RK_LIST , RK_TABLE No with Dv RK_COLUMN , RK_TREE No with Dv RK_ITEM ;
menus, declared by a
.Fa .menu
function
.Pq Dv RK_ITEM , RK_SEPARATOR , RK_HEADING ;
navigation
.Pq Dv RK_PLACES No with Dv RK_PLACE , RK_PIVOT No with Dv RK_PAGE , RK_PANORAMA No with Dv RK_SECTION , RK_SHEET , RK_CONFIRM ;
and
.Dv RK_HOST ,
which puts a view made by hand.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It When it builds
After every event, timer, post and close the library hands the app, after
a look change and at the end of a motion, and before each frame.
Handlers never build themselves; state changed anywhere else calls
.Fn rk_ui_update .
.It Who a node is
Its
.Fa .key
or
.Fa .id
.Pq Dv RK_ID Ns Pq n No for a number from 0
among its siblings, else its place among its unkeyed siblings of its kind.
What comes and goes in a list wants a key, so it keeps its focus, its text
and its motion.
.It Values
A value bound by a pointer
.Pq Fa .on , .chosen , .number , .day , .buffer , .open
is what the view shows after every build, and the user's change is written
there first.
A value only given
.Pq Fa .value , .input
is where the view starts; it is set again only when the declaration moves
it, and
.Fa .on_change
tells the app what the user did.
.It Strings and pointers
Strings and arrays are copied when the macro runs: a local buffer, or
.Fn rk_ui_fmt ,
which formats into memory that lasts until the build ends, is fine.
Bound pointers,
.Fa .user
and
.Fa .data
are kept until the next build declares the node again; in a scope skipped by
.Fa .version ,
until it is built again.
.It Skipping work
A scope whose
.Fa .version
is the same as last time is not built again; a list's or a table's rows are
drawn again only when its version changes.
.It A product's own
A tile's and a row's
.Fa .code
draws a domain colour
.Pq see Fn rk_app_set_series ;
a pivot's
.Fa .wide
is the width from which its pages stand side by side
.Pq see Fn rk_pivot_set_wide .
.It Mistakes
A field the kind does not read, a key twice among siblings, a node the user
closed that the build declares open again: each is said once through the log.
.El
.Pp
A change takes the motion its kind means; a node's
.Fa .change
picks another
.Pq Dv RK_UI_PEEK , RK_UI_FLIP , RK_UI_CROSSFADE ,
and on a container
.Dv RK_UI_IN_PLACE
makes what its scope holds come, go and move at once.
.Pp
.Fn rk_ui_view
puts a declared view into a tree made by hand.
.Fn rk_ui_open ,
.Fn rk_ui_close
and
.Fn rk_ui_put
are what the macros call, with an
.Vt rk_ui
filled in by hand.
.Ss Who owns what
Objects are reference counted, and one rule says who owns them:
.Bl -bullet
.It
A constructor returns a reference you own.
It is named after the thing it makes:
.Fn rk_label ,
.Fn rk_tile ,
.Fn rk_vstack .
Where the thing is a type of its own it is called
.Fn *_create :
.Fn rk_window_create ,
.Fn rk_view_create ,
.Fn rk_controller_create .
.It
Every call that puts a view or controller somewhere takes that reference
over: adding it to a view, a panorama, a pivot, places, a scroll, a split or
a menu slot, showing it in a window, presenting it, giving it to
.Fn rk_controller_create_with_view .
What you put into the tree belongs to the tree; you never release it.
.It
A view that is already in a tree moves when you add it elsewhere; there is
nothing to retain.
.It
A window belongs to the app from
.Fn rk_window_create
until it closes; the pointer you get is borrowed.
.It
Every other pointer you get is borrowed, valid while what it points to is
in the tree or open.
.El
.Pp
.Fn rk_retain
keeps an object beyond that and
.Fn rk_release
lets it go; both report a pointer that is no Rekta object, or one already
freed
.Pq see Sx ERRORS .
.Pp
.Fn rk_add
returns the child it added, so a view can be made, added and kept in one
line \(em keep only what you will change later:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
rk_view *tiles = rk_add(page, rk_tile_grid());
builds = rk_add(tiles, rk_tile("312", "finished today"));
rk_add(tiles, rk_tile("51", "icons"));
.Ed
.Ss App
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fn rk_app_main "const rk_app_desc *desc"
Runs the app until it quits and returns the exit code.
.Fa desc
names the app and gives the
.Fa launch
callback, where the first window is made.
Set
.Fa desc->size
to
.Li sizeof(rk_app_desc) .
.It Fn rk_app_quit "int code"
Ends the loop.
.It Fn rk_app_post "void (*fn)(void *user)" "void *user"
From any thread: runs
.Fa fn
on the main thread soon.
.It Fn rk_app_after "double ms" "void (*fn)(void *user)" "void *user" , Fn rk_app_every "double ms" "void (*fn)(void *user)" "void *user"
A timer that runs once, or again and again; each returns an id, never 0,
for
.Fn rk_app_cancel ,
which also ends a change listener.
.It Fn rk_app_now
Milliseconds on a monotonic clock.
.It Fn rk_app_notify "const char *title" "const char *body"
A notification from the system, with the app's
.Fa name ;
false where there is none, or with no title.
On Linux it goes to the notification server over D-Bus
.Pq the app's Fa id No as its desktop entry ;
on Windows it is a balloon from an icon in the notification area, kept there
while the app runs; on macOS it comes from the notification centre, which asks
the person once, and only for an app in a bundle.
.It Fn rk_app_set_theme , Fn rk_app_set_density , Fn rk_app_set_motion , Fn rk_app_set_accent
Each follows the system until set.
A change of theme or accent cross-fades the windows.
.Fn rk_app_on_change
tells you when any of them changed and returns an id for
.Fn rk_app_cancel ;
.Fn rk_app_is_dark
and
.Fn rk_app_is_reduced_motion
say how things stand.
.It Fn rk_app_set_series "int code" "rk_color light" "rk_color dark"
A domain's colour code, as the web's
.Li --rk-series-1
to
.Li -8 :
colours the users already read, such as heart-rate zones, for small marks
only \(em a tile's sparkline
.Pq Fn rk_tile_set_code ,
a row's tag square
.Pq Fn rk_row_set_code ,
a custom view's marks through
.Fn rk_series_of
\(em never text and never a fill.
.Fa code
is 1 to 8; per theme, as the accent: a
.Fa dark
of 0 takes
.Fa light ,
and 0, 0 is ink again, which is also what a code without a value draws.
Each value should hold 3:1 on bg and surface and stand 0.09 apart in OKLab
under protanopia and deuteranopia from every other code set, as
.Li build.mjs --check-product
holds the web's; one that does not is still set, and said through the log.
.It Fn rk_app_set_metric "rk_metric metric" "float px" , Fn rk_app_set_timing "rk_duration timing" "double ms"
What a product may change of the design
.Pq docs/platforms.md, Product overrides :
the metrics
.Dv RK_TILE_UNIT
and
.Dv RK_PIVOT_WIDE
.Pq the width from which a pivot shows its pages side by side, 1300; 0: never ,
the timings
.Dv RK_TIMING_TOAST
.Pq how long a toast stays, and so the undo window
and
.Dv RK_TIMING_STALE
.Pq when live data without an update is stale, for the app to show .
Below 0 gives back the token's value.
Motion, colours and the type scale stay the design's: any other metric or
duration is said through the log and not set.
.Fn rk_metric_of
and
.Fn rk_duration_of
read what holds now.
.It Fn rk_app_set_strings "const rk_strings *strings"
The words the library says itself, as the web's
.Li Rekta.strings :
a toast's
.Fa undo
and
.Fa redo ,
.Fa undone
.Pq Qq Undone: %s ,
a confirmation's
.Fa ok
and
.Fa cancel ,
a select's
.Fa choose ,
a split's
.Fa back ,
a grip's
.Fa move
.Pq Qq move %s
and a toggle's
.Fa on
and
.Fa off .
Copied; a field left
.Dv NULL
keeps the English word, a format needs exactly one
.Li %s ,
and
.Dv NULL
gives back the English words.
Set
.Fa strings->size
to
.Li sizeof(rk_strings) .
.It Fn rk_app_set_log "void (*fn)(rk_log_level level, const char *message, void *user)" "void *user"
Where misuse is said
.Pq see Sx ERRORS ;
standard error until set.
.It Fn rk_app_add_font "const char *path"
A font file for the characters Barlow lacks, tried before the system's
fonts; false when it cannot be read or drawn with.
Without it a character Barlow lacks comes from the system: fontconfig on
Linux, CoreText on macOS (both opened at run time), the system's fonts on
Windows in an order that suits the user's language.
Lines break at spaces, after a hyphen, and between CJK characters, never
before closing punctuation or after opening.
Text is set a character at a time, left to right: scripts that join or
reorder (Arabic, Hebrew, the Indic scripts) are not shaped yet.
.El
.Ss Windows
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fn rk_window_create "const rk_window_desc *desc"
Makes a window of
.Fa desc->width
by
.Fa desc->height
logical pixels, with Rekta's title bar unless
.Fa desc->system_frame .
With
.Fa desc->build
it shows what that function declares
.Pq Sx Declared UI ,
called with
.Fa desc->user ;
.Fa desc->on_key
hears the keys nothing focused takes.
.It Fn rk_window_show "rk_window *window" "rk_controller *root"
Shows the window with
.Fa root
in it, and takes
.Fa root .
Called again, it puts another root in.
.It Fn rk_window_close , Fn rk_window_on_close
Closes it; the handler may refuse a close the user asked for.
The app ends when its last window closes.
.It Fn rk_window_toast "rk_window *window" "const char *message" "const char *action" "void (*on_action)(void *)" "void *user"
A short message from the top edge; with an
.Fa action
(undo, retry) it stays a little longer.
While focus or the pointer is on it, it stays; Escape dismisses it, and
focus goes back to where it came from.
.It Fn rk_window_do "rk_window *window" "const rk_action *action"
Runs
.Fa action->run
and keeps the action in the window's history (the last 50), with a toast
that says
.Fa action->label
and offers undo; each window has its own.
.Fa action->free_user
is called once the history lets the action go.
A new action drops what was undone.
Set
.Fa action->size
to
.Li sizeof(rk_action) :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
rk_action a = { .size = sizeof a, .label = "archived 3 builds",
                .run = archive, .undo = unarchive, .user = builds };
rk_window_do(window, &a);
.Ed
.It Fn rk_window_undo , Fn rk_window_redo
Steps back or forward through the history, with a toast; false when
there is nothing to step to.
Ctrl (Cmd on macOS) and Z undoes, with Shift (or Ctrl+Y) redoes, except in
a text field, which keeps a history of its own, a word at a time.
.It Fn rk_window_set_title , Fn rk_window_set_size , Fn rk_window_size , Fn rk_window_scale , Fn rk_window_focus
As named.
Sizes are logical pixels; the scale is device pixels per logical pixel.
.It Fn rk_window_choose_files "rk_window *window" "const char *accept" "rk_choose choose" "void (*chosen)(rk_window *window, const char *const *files, int count, void *user)" "void *user"
The system's file chooser over the window, with the filter
.Fn rk_drop
uses,
.Dv RK_CHOOSE_ONE
or
.Dv RK_CHOOSE_MANY ;
.Fa chosen
comes later with the paths, or with none if it was cancelled.
On Linux this is the desktop portal, else
.Xr zenity 1
or kdialog; on Windows the open dialog; on macOS an open panel as a sheet.
False when the system has none here.
.El
.Ss Containers and views
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fn rk_vstack , Fn rk_hstack , Fn rk_tile_grid
Containers with the spacing the design gives them: a column top to
bottom, a row left to right, a grid of tiles in tile units.
A column stretches its children to its width, except buttons, which keep
their own.
.It Fn rk_add "rk_view *parent" "rk_view *child"
Adds
.Fa child
at the end, takes it, and returns it; a child that has a parent moves.
.Fn rk_insert
puts it at an index.
.It Fn rk_view_remove "rk_view *child"
Takes it out of its parent; it is gone unless you retained it.
Inside an
.Dv RK_MOTION_LEAVE
block it leaves first and the rest close up.
.It Fn rk_view_reorder "rk_view *view" "rk_view *const *children" "int count"
Puts the children in that order, without taking them out: focus stays, and
inside an
.Dv RK_MOTION_REFLOW
block they glide there.
.It Fn rk_label "const char *text" "rk_text_style style"
Text that wraps at its width, in one of the type roles
.Pf ( Dv RK_TEXT_BODY ,
.Dv RK_TEXT_GROUP ,
.Dv RK_TEXT_TITLE ,
\&...).
.It Fn rk_button "const char *label" "void (*on_press)(rk_view *, void *)" "void *user"
A button.
.Fn rk_button_set_kind
makes it primary, quiet or a bar button;
.Fn rk_button_set_icon
gives it an icon
.Pq Sx ICONS ;
a button with an icon alone is named by it.
Every view's text changes in place:
.Fn rk_label_set_style ,
.Fn rk_button_set_label ,
.Fn rk_control_set_label ,
.Fn rk_control_set_options ,
.Fn rk_control_set_range ,
.Fn rk_field_set_label ,
.Fn rk_field_set_placeholder ,
.Fn rk_icon_set .
.It Fn rk_tile "const char *value" "const char *label"
A tile: a value and what it counts.
.Fn rk_tile_set_status
fills it with a status;
.Fn rk_tile_set_stat ,
.Fn rk_tile_set_message ,
.Fn rk_tile_set_series
and
.Fn rk_tile_set_progress
change what it shows;
.Fn rk_tile_set_code
draws its sparkline in a domain colour.
A tile with an activate handler presses and opens something.
.It Fn rk_rows , Fn rk_rows_add
Hairline rows: a title on the left, a meta or a status on the right.
A row is a view:
.Fn rk_row_set
changes it,
.Fn rk_row_set_code
makes its right side a tag whose square takes a domain colour, the words in
ink,
.Fn rk_insert
moves it,
.Fn rk_view_remove
takes it out, and it presses, has a tooltip or a menu like any other.
.It Fn rk_rows_set_sortable "rk_view *rows" "void (*moved)(rk_view *rows, int from, int to, void *user)" "void *user"
Rows the user puts in order: each gets a grip at its leading edge.
Dragged 4 pixels up or down, a row lifts (the background and a 1 pixel edge)
and follows the pointer while the others step aside; let go, every row that
moved glides to its place.
On the grip, Space or Enter picks the row up, Up and Down move it, Space or
Enter puts it down and Escape puts it back; Alt with Up or Down moves it at
once.
.Fa moved
hears every move, to keep the app's order and its undo;
.Fn rk_rows_move
moves a row from the app.
.It Fn rk_menu
A menu.
.Fn rk_menu_add
adds an item \(em a lowercase verb or option, a key hint, a handler \(em
and returns its index;
.Fn rk_menu_add_separator
and
.Fn rk_menu_add_label
group them.
.Fn rk_menu_set_check
gives an item a square
.Pf ( Dv RK_CHECKMARK_OFF ,
.Dv RK_CHECKMARK_ON )
that a choice toggles while the menu stays open;
.Fn rk_menu_set_enabled
greys one out, and
.Fn rk_menu_clear
empties the menu to build it again.
.Fn rk_view_set_menu
attaches it: a button opens it when pressed, any other view on a right
click or Shift+F10.
.Fn rk_menu_open
and
.Fn rk_menu_open_at
open it by hand,
.Fn rk_menu_close
closes it; it floats below what opened it, above where there is no
room, never outside the window.
Keys: Up and Down, Home and End, a letter, Enter or Space, Escape; Tab or
a press outside closes it.
.Fn rk_menu_opener
tells a handler which view the menu was for.
.It Fn rk_image "const char *path" , Fn rk_image_from_memory "const void *data" "size_t size"
A PNG or JPEG picture, from a file or from bytes (copied).
Its size is known at once, from the header
.Pq Fn rk_image_size ;
it is decoded away from the UI thread and fades in where a quiet
placeholder stood.
It shows whole
.Pq Dv RK_FIT_CONTAIN
or fills its view
.Pq Dv RK_FIT_COVER ,
its shape kept
.Pq Fn rk_image_set_fit ;
drawn at a size, it is scaled once with a filter that averages every
pixel it shrinks, and a big picture is scaled away from the UI thread
too.
Its natural size is its pixels, smaller where it has less room.
.It Fn rk_scroll "rk_view *content"
Clips
.Fa content
and scrolls it up and down on the panorama's spring: the wheel until an
end (then what holds it moves), the keys a focused view inside leaves
(Up, Down, Page Up, Page Down, Home, End), and focus landing inside,
which brings the view in sight.
A 4px bar shows while it moves and a moment after; it drags.
.Fn rk_view_reveal
brings a view in sight in every scroll it is in.
.It Fn rk_check "const char *label" "bool on" , Fn rk_toggle "const char *label" "bool on"
A checkbox (Space toggles) and the Metro toggle, whose state word stands
beside it; its thumb moves over duration-short.
.It Fn rk_choices "const char *const *options" "int count" "int chosen" , Fn rk_segments "const char *const *options" "int count" "int chosen"
One of several options: a group of circles, one stop for Tab, the arrows
choose
.Pq Fa chosen No -1 for none yet ;
or two to five segments side by side, the accent sliding to the choice.
.It Fn rk_slider "double min" "double max" "double step" "double value" , Fn rk_stepper "double min" "double max" "double step" "double value"
A number in a range: a track the accent fills up to the value, with the
value beside it (a press moves it there, a drag follows); or minus, the
number and plus (a held press repeats; typing a number and Enter, or
leaving, takes it; Escape puts it back).
Both snap to
.Fa step
(0: none) and know Up, Down, Page Up, Page Down, Home and End.
.It Fn rk_select "const char *const *options" "int count" "int chosen"
A box with the chosen option and a chevron: a press, Enter, Space or
Alt+Down opens a menu of the options below it, the chosen one marked;
Up and Down change it in place, a letter jumps to the next option
starting with it.
.It Fn rk_date "int year" "int month" "int day"
A date, shown as 2026-10-13; its value is the number 20261013 (0 for none
yet, from
.Fn rk_date 0 0 0 ) .
Left and Right go between year, month and day, Up and Down change the
one in focus, digits type it.
The calendar, Alt+Down or Enter opens the month below it, Monday first:
the arrows go by day and week, Page Up and Page Down by month (with
Shift, by year), Home and End to the month's ends, Enter takes a day,
Escape closes.
.It Fn rk_control_value , Fn rk_control_set_value , Fn rk_control_on_change
Every control holds a number: 0 or 1, the chosen index, the number, or
the date as yyyymmdd
.Po
.Fn rk_date_value
and
.Fn rk_date_set_value
take it apart
.Pc .
A number snaps to the range and step; an index the control lacks is
ignored; the change handler hears
what the user changes, not what the app sets.
.It Fn rk_field "const char *label" "const char *placeholder"
A text field: a label above, the input, a hint below.
One line, edited with the desktop's keys (arrows, Ctrl by word, Shift to
select, Home, End, Backspace, Delete, Ctrl+A, Ctrl+C, Ctrl+X, Ctrl+V) and
the pointer (a click places the caret, a drag selects, a double click
takes a word).
.Fn rk_field_text
and
.Fn rk_field_set_text
read and write it;
.Fn rk_field_set_hint
shows a hint below it,
.Fn rk_field_set_error
the cause and remedy of an error, marked, in its place;
.Fn rk_field_set_secret
makes it a password, its text shown as dots and never copied or read
aloud;
.Fn rk_field_on_change
follows every edit and
.Fn rk_field_on_submit
Enter.
Text an input method composes shows underlined at the caret
.Pq Dv RK_EVENT_COMPOSE .
The caret blinks for ten seconds after an edit, then stays lit.
Ctrl+Z and Ctrl+Shift+Z step through the field's own history, a word at
a time.
.It Fn rk_field_set_lines "rk_view *field" "int lines"
Shows
.Fa lines
lines: a text area.
The text wraps at the input's width, at spaces; Enter breaks a line and
Ctrl+Enter submits; Up and Down keep to the column they started from,
Home and End to the line, Ctrl+Home and Ctrl+End go to either end.
Pasted line breaks are kept as
.Ql \en .
.It Fn rk_field_set_options "rk_view *field" "const char *const *options" "int count"
Makes the field a combobox: the options that contain what is typed float
below the input without taking focus; Down opens the list or moves down
it, Up moves up, Enter takes the active option, Escape closes it.
What is typed need not be an option; taking one is an edit Ctrl+Z
takes back.
.It Fn rk_list "int count" "void (*item)(int index, rk_list_item *out, void *user)" "void *user"
A long list.
Only the rows in sight are drawn and nothing is kept for each item:
.Fa item
fills a row's title, meta and status while it is drawn.
.Fn rk_list_set_count
says the items changed.
With
.Fn rk_list_set_key ,
which gives each item a key of its own, the selection and the active row
follow their items when they move or go; without it they keep to their
places.
A click selects one and calls the
.Fn rk_list_on_show
handler; Ctrl and a click toggles and starts selecting several, Shift and
a click takes a range, Escape ends it;
.Fn rk_list_on_change
hears what the user changes.
.Fn rk_list_is_selected ,
.Fn rk_list_selection_count ,
.Fn rk_list_selection
and
.Fn rk_list_active
read it;
.Fn rk_list_select
and
.Fn rk_list_clear_selection
set it from the app.
.Fn rk_list_scroll_to
glides a row into sight.
.It Fn rk_drop "const char *title" "const char *hint" "const char *accept" "void (*files)(rk_view *drop, const char *const *taken, int ntaken, const char *const *refused, int nrefused, void *user)" "void *user"
A place to drop files: a dashed edge that turns solid while files are
dragged over it.
.Fa accept
filters them by extension
.Pq Qq .png,.jpg
or by family
.Pq Qq image/* ;
NULL takes any.
.Fa files
hears the files taken and those refused, only when one was taken.
A click or Enter opens the system's file chooser
.Pq Fn rk_window_choose_files
with the same filter.
Files dropped where no view takes them are refused.
.It Fn rk_tree void
Items that hold items, opened and closed in place.
.Fn rk_tree_add "tree" "parent" "title"
adds an item under
.Fa parent
(\-1 at the top) and returns its number;
.Fn rk_tree_set_meta
puts a meta or a status on its right;
.Fn rk_tree_move
puts an item before another of its siblings, or at their end.
A click, Enter or Space selects an item and calls the
.Fn rk_tree_on_show
handler; selection never follows focus.
A click on the chevron or a double click opens or closes a branch, which
glides open over its height;
.Fn rk_tree_on_toggle
hears it,
.Fn rk_tree_set_open
and
.Fn rk_tree_set_all_open
do it.
Up and Down move, Right opens or goes in, Left closes or goes up, * opens
every sibling, a letter goes to the next item starting with it.
.Fn rk_tree_select ,
.Fn rk_tree_selected ,
.Fn rk_tree_is_open ,
.Fn rk_tree_parent ,
.Fn rk_tree_title ,
.Fn rk_tree_set_title ,
.Fn rk_tree_user
and
.Fn rk_tree_set_user
read and set the rest.
.Fn rk_tree_remove
takes an item out with everything under it (its number is not given
again);
.Fn rk_tree_clear
empties the tree.
.It Fn rk_split "rk_view *list" "rk_view *detail" "const char *title"
A list beside its detail; takes both.
Sideways (the split 760 logical pixels wide or more, the window 481 tall or
more) the two stand side by side with a hairline between them, and its handle
drags the list's width: no pane narrower than pane-min, four tiles wide at
first; Left and Right change it by space-m, with Shift four times that, Home
and End go to the narrowest and widest, a double click gives back the list's
own width.
.Fn rk_split_on_resize
hears a change, to remember;
.Fn rk_split_set_width
gives it back.
Compact one pane shows at a time:
.Fn rk_split_set_detail_shown
turns to the detail, whose crumb
.Pq Fa title
and Escape turn back, which
.Fn rk_split_on_back
hears; sideways it slides a new item's detail in.
.Fn rk_split_is_sideways ,
.Fn rk_split_is_detail_shown
and
.Fn rk_split_width
read the state.
Each pane scrolls itself: give one that may be long an
.Fn rk_scroll .
.It Fn rk_table "int count" "const char *(*cell)(int row, int column, void *user)" "void *user"
A table: a list with columns, and every
.Fn rk_list_*
call works on it.
.Fn rk_table_add_column
adds a column with a title, a width
.Pf ( Dv RK_AUTO
shares what is left) and flags:
.Dv RK_COLUMN_NUMERIC
sets it to the right,
.Dv RK_COLUMN_SORTABLE
lets a click on its head sort by it, ascending then descending.
.Fn rk_table_insert_column ,
.Fn rk_table_remove_column
and
.Fn rk_table_set_column
change the columns where they stand.
The app sorts its rows in the
.Fn rk_table_on_sort
handler, which hears the column and an
.Vt rk_sort ;
the table draws them again.
.Fn rk_table_set_sort
shows the sort the rows already have.
The head stays put while the rows scroll.
A column's edge in the head drags its width, never below 48; a double
click on it gives the column its own width back.
.Fn rk_table_set_column_width
does the same from the app (0: its own), and
.Fn rk_table_column_width
says what it is now, to remember it.
.It Fn rk_table_on_edit "rk_view *table" "const char *(*commit)(rk_view *table, int row, int column, const char *value, void *user)" "void *user"
Cells in a column with
.Dv RK_COLUMN_EDITABLE
open on a double click or F2 (the active row's first such cell), or
.Fn rk_table_edit :
a field over the cell, with the old value selected.
Enter or Tab keeps the value (Tab goes on to the next editable cell,
Shift+Tab back), Escape puts the old one back, focus going elsewhere
keeps it.
.Fa commit
returns
.Dv NULL
to take the value, or why not: the reason shows below the cell, which
stays open.
.It Fn rk_icon , Fn rk_progress
An icon from the set
.Pq Sx ICONS ;
a progress bar, indeterminate below zero, moved by
.Fn rk_progress_set_value .
.It Fn rk_view_on_activate "rk_view *view" "void (*fn)(rk_view *, void *)" "void *user"
Called with the view when it is pressed or activated from the keyboard.
.It Fn rk_view_find "rk_view *root" "const char *name"
The view with that name
.Pq Fn rk_view_set_name ,
or a button by its label, or its icon when it has no label.
.It Fn rk_view_set_enabled "rk_view *view" "bool enabled"
Any view: a disabled one is greyed and takes no press, and so is
everything inside it; it leaves the Tab order unless it has a tooltip, which
says why;
.Fn rk_view_is_enabled
says whether it or a view holding it is disabled.
.It Fn rk_view_set_tooltip "rk_view *view" "const char *text"
A short text that floats near the view after the pointer rests on it a
moment, or when it takes focus from the keyboard.
It repeats what the view says, never the only way to learn it.
.It Fn rk_view_set_cursor , Fn rk_view_set_user
The pointer's shape over the view; the app's own data, with a function
that lets it go when the view does.
.It Fn rk_view_set_padding , Fn rk_view_set_gap , Fn rk_view_set_align , Fn rk_view_set_grow , Fn rk_view_set_size , Fn rk_view_set_span
Layout, where the defaults do not fit.
.It Fn rk_view_set_fill , Fn rk_view_set_radius , Fn rk_view_set_rules , Fn rk_view_set_opacity , Fn rk_view_set_hidden
Look, by role so it follows the theme;
.Fn rk_view_set_rules
takes
.Dv RK_RULE_TOP
and
.Dv RK_RULE_BOTTOM .
.It Fn rk_view_create_custom "const rk_view_class *cls" "void *state"
A view of your own: a class with
.Fa draw ,
.Fa measure ,
.Fa layout
and
.Fa event
callbacks, drawing with the
.Fn rk_canvas_*
functions.
.Fn rk_view_animate_value
